# Answer key for B2 t1_breadth.sql - subqueries, CTEs, set operations: breadth.
#
# WRITTEN FROM SOURCE BEFORE THE ANALYSER WAS RUN OVER THIS FILE.
#
# Conventions: B1's (D-2, D-4, D-5, D-6, D-7, D-9, D-10, D-11), convention (a) for set
# operations, plus D-12 and D-13 decided for this batch and NOT yet implemented - the facts
# resting on them are expected to score as disagreements until they are.

@ 18 S1 uncorrelated scalar subquery
value dim_store.name -> kpi_rollup.region identity
value fct_order_line.net_amount, fct_order_line.status -> kpi_rollup.revenue aggregated
note D-12: status decides which lines feed the total; every store row is written.
forbid filter fct_order_line.status -> kpi_rollup | a SELECT-list scalar subquery's WHERE removes no target row (D-12)

@ 34 S2 correlated aggregating scalar subqueries
value customers.id -> customer_summary.customer_id identity
value customers.full_name -> customer_summary.full_name identity
value payments.amount, payments.status, orders.customer_id, customers.id -> customer_summary.lifetime_value aggregated
value orders.customer_id, customers.id -> customer_summary.lifetime_orders aggregated
note COUNT(*) has no value source; only its correlation feeds lifetime_orders (D-12).
forbid filter payments.status -> customer_summary | inside a SELECT-list scalar subquery: a value of lifetime_value (D-12)
forbid filter customers.id -> customer_summary | a correlation operand of a SELECT-list scalar subquery (D-12)
forbid filter orders.customer_id -> customer_summary | a correlation operand of a SELECT-list scalar subquery (D-12)
forbid filter payments.order_id -> customer_summary | the join inside the subquery is structural (D-5), and the subquery filters nothing anyway

@ 55 S3 lookup scalar subqueries
value orders.id -> top_customers.customer_id identity
value customers.full_name -> top_customers.full_name identity
value customers.id, orders.customer_id -> top_customers.full_name conditional
value dim_store.region -> top_customers.region identity
value dim_store.id, orders.store_id -> top_customers.region conditional
note D-12: a lookup's keys select which value arrives - conditional, like a CASE condition.
filter orders.channel -> top_customers
forbid filter dim_store.id -> top_customers | a lookup key in a SELECT-list scalar subquery removes no row (D-12)
forbid filter orders.store_id -> top_customers | a lookup key in a SELECT-list scalar subquery removes no row (D-12)

@ 68 S4 an ordered pick (D-13)
value shipments.order_id -> customer_summary.customer_id identity
value payments.amount -> customer_summary.lifetime_value identity
value payments.order_id, shipments.order_id -> customer_summary.lifetime_value conditional
influence payments.paid_at -> customer_summary.lifetime_value
forbid filter payments.paid_at -> customer_summary | ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1 in a scalar subquery picks a value, it removes no row (D-13)
forbid value payments.paid_at -> customer_summary.lifetime_value | paid_at decides which amount, no part of it is in the amount (D-13)

@ 85 S5 EXISTS and NOT EXISTS
value customers.id -> top_customers.customer_id identity
value customers.segment -> top_customers.region identity
filter orders.customer_id, customers.id, orders.status -> top_customers
note Both subqueries are in WHERE: they decide which customers land. The NOT EXISTS reaches orders.customer_id through two structural joins.
forbid filter order_lines.id -> top_customers | a join key inside the NOT EXISTS (D-5)

@ 112 S6 IN and NOT IN
value orders.channel -> channel_mix.channel identity
value orders.currency -> channel_mix.region identity
value orders.amount -> channel_mix.revenue identity
filter orders.customer_id, customers.id, customers.segment, orders.store_id, dim_store.id, dim_store.status -> channel_mix

@ 131 S7 ALL, ANY, a scalar comparison in WHERE
value fct_order_line.order_date -> sales_daily.sale_date identity
value fct_order_line.store_id -> sales_daily.store_id identity
value fct_order_line.net_amount -> sales_daily.net_amount identity
filter fct_order_line.net_amount, returns.amount, returns.status, fct_order_line.product_key, dim_product.product_key, dim_product.brand, fct_order_line.unit_price -> sales_daily
filter dim_product.list_price -> sales_daily post
note list_price reaches its comparison through AVG: post-aggregation, per column (D-6).

@ 151 S8 an aggregating derived table joined back
value fct_order_line.product_key -> product_performance.product_id identity
value fct_order_line.net_amount -> product_performance.revenue identity
value fct_order_line.gross_amount -> product_performance.revenue_share derived
value fct_order_line.net_amount -> product_performance.revenue_share aggregated
influence fct_order_line.store_id -> product_performance.revenue_share

@ 171 S9 CROSS JOIN LATERAL, top 3 per store
value dim_store.region -> product_performance.category_name identity
value orders.id -> product_performance.product_id identity
value orders.amount -> product_performance.revenue identity
influence orders.amount -> product_performance.units
filter orders.store_id, dim_store.id, orders.amount -> product_performance
note The LATERAL's correlation decides which rows land (a row source, not a scalar); amount orders the LIMIT 3 (D-9).

@ 195 S10 six CTEs with MATERIALIZED hints
value dim_store.region -> channel_mix.region identity
value fct_order_line.net_amount -> channel_mix.revenue aggregated
influence fct_order_line.store_id, dim_store.region -> channel_mix.revenue
filter fct_order_line.status -> channel_mix
filter fct_order_line.net_amount -> channel_mix post
note `WHERE e.region_rev > 0` filters a SUM of a SUM: post-aggregation (D-6).

@ 238 S11 WITH RECURSIVE over the category tree
value dim_category.name -> kpi_rollup.level derived
value dim_category.department -> kpi_rollup.region identity
value dim_category.name -> kpi_rollup.segment identity
filter dim_category.parent_id -> kpi_rollup
note The anchor's `parent_id IS NULL` picks the roots. `depth` (-> orders) has no base source: a literal that only adds to itself.
forbid value dim_category.id -> kpi_rollup.orders | depth counts recursion steps; the join key is not in it

@ 257 S12a UNION ALL, both arms supply values
value orders.channel, payments.method -> sales_daily.region identity
value orders.amount, payments.amount -> sales_daily.net_amount identity
filter orders.status -> sales_daily

@ 271 S12b INTERSECT then EXCEPT ALL
value orders.customer_id -> top_customers.customer_id identity
filter dim_customer.customer_id, dim_customer.segment, returns.order_line_id, returns.status -> top_customers
note Convention (a): the later arms of INTERSECT and EXCEPT decide which values survive and supply none.
forbid value dim_customer.customer_id -> top_customers.customer_id | an INTERSECT's later arm supplies no value (convention (a))

@ 289 S12c a parenthesised UNION, then EXCEPT, in a CTE
value dim_store.region, dim_warehouse.region -> kpi_rollup.region identity
value dim_store.country_code -> kpi_rollup.country_code identity
filter dim_customer.region, dim_customer.country_code, dim_customer.is_current -> kpi_rollup
forbid filter dim_store.region -> kpi_rollup | UNION (distinct) removes only duplicates of the values written (D-9's DISTINCT rule)

@ 310 S12d ORDER BY / LIMIT over a whole UNION ALL
value payments.order_id, returns.order_line_id -> top_customers.customer_id identity
value payments.amount, returns.amount -> top_customers.revenue identity
filter payments.amount, returns.amount -> top_customers
note D-9: ORDER BY 2 is the second output column, which both arms supply.

@ 323 S13 bare SELECT with a correlated scalar subquery
value dim_warehouse.name -> <result>.warehouse_name identity
value inventory_snapshots.on_hand, inventory_snapshots.warehouse_id, dim_warehouse.id -> <result>.stock_units aggregated
forbid filter inventory_snapshots.warehouse_id -> <result> | a SELECT-list scalar subquery's correlation removes no row (D-12)
